.TH BEAMFS\-BENCH 1 "2026-10-09" "beamfs development tools" "beamfs development tools"
.SH NAME
beamfs\-bench \- measure beamfs under fault injection, and prove the lab
was in a known state when it did
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B beamfs\-bench
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs resilience and performance measurements against beamfs on a cluster
of four VMs, under live fault injection, and writes a GPG\-signed
manifest of what ran and what the lab looked like while it ran.
.PP
The manifest is the point as much as the numbers. A measurement whose
conditions nobody recorded cannot be defended, and a filesystem that
claims to survive radiation has to be able to say what it survived and
on what. Every run writes one to
.I Documentation/runs/
in the yocto layer, signed.
.PP
Before any measurement the pipeline verifies the lab itself: the VM disk
architecture against the isolation contract, every USB stick by writing
to it and reading it back, the source tree against its mirror, and the
code through every static analyser installed. A run that starts on an
unverified lab produces numbers nobody can use \(em on 2026-09-11 the USB
pre\-flight caught a stick that acknowledged a 4 MiB write at 42 MB/s
and left 213009 bytes of the old contents in place. It was one of five
slots feeding the multi\-filesystem comparison.
.IP
Since 0.14.1 the USB pre-flight wipes each slot and reads it back in
O_DIRECT, so that the read comes from the medium and not from the page
cache that the wipe just filled; and a slot that reads back anything
but zeros is reported with the count of non-zero bytes, the offset of
the first, its first 32 bytes, and the running domain the device is
attached to, if any.
.IP
Since 0.14.2 the module version the lockstep phases compare against is
read from the layer, the highest recipes-kernel/beamfs/files/beamfs-<v>
directory, unless BEAMFS_BENCH_MODULE_VERSION is set; it was a default
of 0.1.3, and a clean run failed on it once the layer had moved on. The
domain a slot is attached to is found by comparing what the paths point
at, not the paths themselves.
.IP
Since 0.14.3 the image the pipeline builds and treats as canonical is
beamfs-research-image, read from
tmp/deploy/images/<machine>/beamfs-research-image-<machine>.rootfs.beamfs;
the default had stayed hpc-arm64-research-beamfs, which the layer no
longer builds, and phase 0.3 stopped on it. BEAMFS_BENCH_IMAGE still
overrides the name.
.IP
Since 0.14.4 the scrub scenario (Test G) reads the damaged block back
raw from the medium, volume unmounted and in O_DIRECT, before the
injection, after it and after the sweeps, and reports how many bytes
the injection changed, how many are still different after the sweeps,
and whether the block on the medium is the clean one again. The digest
of the file, all it reported about the data until then, goes through a
read, which corrects in memory, and holds with or without a repair.
--node and --device run it on another node than compute01, x86-01
included, with BEAMFS_BENCH_SSH_KEY and BEAMFS_BENCH_SSH_USER for that
node. full refuses a --machine other than the cluster's (qemuarm64, or
BEAMFS_BENCH_CLUSTER_MACHINE): the cluster's domains are aarch64, and
an x86-64 image deployed on them boots nothing.
.IP
Since 0.14.5 the key that reaches the lab is by default the layer's
recipes-core/images/files/beamfs-release, the one beamfs-research-image
authorizes for hpcadmin and root; it was ~/.ssh/hpclab_admin, which the
image refuses, and every phase that waits for SSH timed out on domains
that were up. BEAMFS_BENCH_SSH_KEY still overrides it. When the wait
for SSH times out, the error says what ssh answered on the last
attempt.
.IP
Since 0.14.6 the manifest of a pipeline is signed only when a key is in
gpg-agent's cache at that moment, and gpg is given two minutes: otherwise
the manifest is kept unsigned and the reason is printed with the command
that signs it. The signature is written to a .part file and renamed once
gpg has succeeded, so no empty or partial .asc is left. When session
priming cannot cache the key it says so, instead of reporting every
credential primed.
.IP
Since 0.15.0 the code analysis runs checkpatch as a reviewer does: the
checkpatch of the kernel repository named by BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_REPO,
~/git/linux by default, on the module sources laid out as one patch,
with spdxcheck, and every ERROR and WARNING refuses the run. It was the
host kernel's checkpatch with \-\-no\-tree on \-\-file, where a line over
100 columns is a CHECK, counting ERROR lines only: the ten long lines of
beamfs 0.1.26 passed every gate until the series was checked by hand on
2026\-10\-09. A python3 that cannot import ply and git made spdxcheck
die without a word; the interpreter is now looked for, through EPYTHON
where python\-exec chooses it, and none found refuses the run.
.B upstream
runs every check a patch series has to pass before it is mailed.
.IP
Since 0.15.1 the built\-in variants of
.B upstream
ask for sysfs and procfs, which tinyconfig leaves out; the first run
had built them without, and said so. make synchronises its output by
target, so that the lines of parallel jobs no longer interleave, and
each diagnostic is listed once, with its count, without the path of
the worktree. get_maintainer lists the recipients from MAINTAINERS
alone: from the git history it had named the last committers of
MAINTAINERS and of fs/Makefile.
.IP
Since 0.16.0
.B upstream
asks for everything Documentation/process/submit\-checklist.rst asks
that can be checked without booting, and for what
Documentation/process/generated\-content.rst and coding\-assistants.rst
ask of a series made with tools: builds on 32 bits, i386 and arm with
LLVM, and big\-endian, s390 with LLVM, each with beamfs as a module so
that modpost sees what a 32\-bit machine cannot link; W=1 on them as
well; gcc \-W (KCFLAGS=\-W) on fs/beamfs; smatch through Kbuild; every
semantic patch of coccicheck in report mode; a cover letter with a
paragraph beginning "Tools" and one beginning "Testing"; and an
Assisted\-by line that may name the analysis tools after its value.
arm64 is no longer built big\-endian: CPU_BIG_ENDIAN depends on BROKEN
there since v7.3. The code analysis takes its checkpatch and its
kernel\-doc from a worktree of BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_BASE, origin/master by
default: they came from the working tree of the kernel repository, on
whatever branch was checked out there, and from /usr/src/linux, the
station's own kernel. Before sparse and smatch run, the tools check
passes each through the tree's scripts/checker\-valid.sh, the test
kbuild applies before C=1 or C=2: kbuild turns a checker it refuses
off, says so in one line, and checks nothing. When modpost finds a
symbol undefined in a module build, the objects of beamfs are
recompiled there with \-g, and each call is placed: file and line,
function, symbol. coccicheck is given the directory of beamfs as an
absolute M=, and runs there; it fails when it ran elsewhere or ran no
semantic patch, and its findings, which it names from that directory,
are reported under fs/beamfs/. The tools check runs spatch on a
semantic patch with a python rule and an OCaml rule: spatch compiles
an OCaml rule with the OCaml compiler on PATH, which has to be the one
coccinelle was built with.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B checkpoint
Say whether this repository, what was pushed, and the installed binary
still describe the same code.
.sp
This tool does not run from its repository. It is emerged, and what
runs is /usr/bin/beamfs-bench, built by the ebuild from the remote. An
edit reaches a measurement only after a commit, a push and an emerge,
and any of the three can be skipped without a word: asked for the first
time, the answer was four files uncommitted, seven commits never
pushed, and a binary forty-nine hours older than the last commit.
.sp
Since 0.13.0 the same links are checked before every measurement and a
broken one refuses the run.
.B BEAMFS_BENCH_FORCE=1
takes it anyway.
.TP
.B upstream
Check a kernel patch series the way its reviewers and their build robots
will, before it is mailed. The series is a branch of the kernel
repository over a base;
.B upstream
formats it itself, with its cover letter, and checks:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the tools it needs, spdxcheck, smatch, spatch and the LLVM tools
included: a tool that cannot run fails its check, and so does a
checker the tree's scripts/checker\-valid.sh refuses;
.IP \(bu 2
that the sources under fs/beamfs are those of \-\-measured, byte for
byte;
.IP \(bu 2
every commit: signed, by \-\-author, with its Signed\-off\-by and one
Assisted\-by line, \-\-assisted\-by alone or followed by the names of
the analysis tools used, without Co\-Authored\-By, its subject within 60
characters once numbered;
.IP \(bu 2
every patch with checkpatch: no error and no warning, FILE_PATH_CHANGES
excepted where the MAINTAINERS entry of the series covers every file it
adds; the \-\-strict CHECKs counted by type, and blocking with
\-\-strict\-blocking;
.IP \(bu 2
every mail under \-\-max\-mail\-bytes; a cover letter without a
placeholder, with its base\-commit, naming the assistance the patches
declare, with a paragraph beginning "Tools" and one beginning
"Testing"; no typographic dash or quote in the cover letter or the
documentation, and nothing but ASCII in the sources;
.IP \(bu 2
MAINTAINERS: the entry at the head of the series, and get_maintainer's
self\-test;
.IP \(bu 2
Kconfig: help text, off by default, sourced from fs/Kconfig and built
from fs/Makefile;
.IP \(bu 2
the userspace interfaces: MODULE_PARM_DESC for each module parameter,
every sysfs attribute in Documentation/ABI, procfs, ioctl numbers and
boot parameters documented, and linux\-api named when there is one;
.IP \(bu 2
builds without a warning: x86_64 tinyconfig with beamfs as a module;
built in with every option, SMP, preemption and tracing; built in under
lockdep and the memory debugging options; built in without sysfs, procfs
and debugfs; with clang; allnoconfig; allmodconfig on fs/beamfs, with
CONFIG_WERROR; arm64 with a cross compiler, unless \-\-no\-cross; i386
and arm with LLVM, 32 bits, and s390 with LLVM, big\-endian, each with
beamfs as a module. Then W=1 on the module, built\-in, clang, i386, arm
and s390 builds, gcc \-W on fs/beamfs, sparse, smatch, coccicheck,
kernel\-doc on every source and header, and checkstack under
\-\-stack\-limit;
.IP \(bu 2
with \-\-same\-text\-as, that the code sections of every object, and
their relocations, are those of that beamfs revision;
.IP \(bu 2
that the series merges into every \-\-newer revision and builds there;
.IP \(bu 2
the documentation build: no warning and no broken file reference that
the base does not have.
.RE
.IP
Writes the patches, the logs, upstream\-summary.json, signed when
gpg\-agent holds the key, and upstream\-report.txt. Exits 0 when every
check passes and 3 otherwise. The runtime half of the submit checklist,
a debug kernel, lockdep, fault injection and linux\-next, belongs to
beamfs\-xfstests, on a kernel the bitbake chain built; it is not
measured here.
.TP
.B full
The whole pipeline: lab verification, VM lifecycle, cluster bootstrap,
then
.B analyse
at full scope. One command, autonomous. Must exit 0 before any commit or
push.
.TP
.B multifs
Head\-to\-head comparison of several filesystems under live injection,
two filesystems and three probabilities by default. Requires the device
validation prompt, or
.BR \-\-auto\-confirm .
.TP
.B perf
Performance characterisation of every filesystem, in two regimes.
.TP
.B analyse
multifs plus forensic capture: dmesg, the injector's counters, ftrace,
perf and the cluster state. Three scopes through
.BR \-\-scope=quick|standard|full .
.sp
Since 0.14.7 each node's cluster attack poses the extents of that node's
own target file, read with filefrag, and its CLUSTER record carries them
as TARGET_RANGES. Before, the list came from the environment, where the
multifs loop had left its last filesystem's extents: on 2026\-10\-05 every
read of /data was filtered out and the twelve cluster cells came out
NOT_EXERCISED. A node whose extents cannot be read skips the attack with
reason=target_extents_unavailable.
.sp
Since 0.14.8 the extent list is merged before it is posed, and every
write of it into emufi is read back. emufi refuses a list longer than 512
bytes and keeps at most 8 ranges; beamfs reports one extent per block, so
its 69\-range lists were refused on 2026\-10\-08 and compute01 stayed under
the list the multifs loop had left (NOT_EXERCISED). Contiguous extents now
make one range. A list over 8 ranges or 512 bytes, or one the module does
not hold after the write, skips the attack with
reason=target_ranges_over_limit or reason=target_ranges_not_applied, the
list cleared. Clearing the list is a real write.
.TP
.B metadata
Test A. Attacks the metadata specifically \(em superblock, inode bitmap,
journal \(em rather than waiting for random injection to reach it.
.TP
.B crash
Test B. Crash consistency: the VM is destroyed mid\-write and remounted.
.TP
.B bitrot
Test C. Offline bit\-rot: random bytes written to an unmounted
partition, then read back.
.TP
.B fsck
Test D. What the offline checker makes of a filesystem after a panic.
.TP
.B tindirect
Test F. Triple\-indirect addressing round trip: sparse writes across the
dindirect\-to\-tindirect frontier, then sync, drop_caches, remount and a
byte\-identical read. No injection; this one validates the addressing
arithmetic and its bounds checks.
.TP
.B scrub
Measures the scrubber itself rather than the read path.
.TP
.B mega
Test E. Everything above into one tarball under
.IR /tmp ,
with the Yocto build logs, the kernel config, modinfo, the git HEADs and
the post\-attack forensics. For handing an investigation to somebody
else, or to yourself in a month.
.TP
.B trace
Attaches a bpftrace script while something happens, on a cluster node
or on the station, and keeps what it printed.
.IP
Every other command here reads state after the fact \(em a dmesg, a
frozen volume, counters sampled before and after. None of that shows a
sequence, and a sequence is what the open defects are made of: a pointer
installed into an indirect block and read back as zero twenty\-five
seconds later by a different task. The trace of a race is not the race.
.IP
The scripts are not in this package. They live in beamfs\-xfstests
under
.IR scripts/ ,
twenty of them, each written against a specific defect, and they are
found by name on a search path rather than copied here \(em two
collections drifting apart would leave the wrong one installed.
.B \-\-list
says which ones were found and what each watches.
.IP
Needs bpftrace on the target. It entered the arm64 image on 2026\-09\-19,
when meta\-clang was added to that build directory; before that the
probes could only run on the station.
.TP
.B db
The measurement database: retroactive ingest of archived runs, and
queries over the accumulated series.
.TP
.B version
Version and build info.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-bell
Finish without ringing. Accepted on any subcommand. See
.B THE BELL
below.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-confirm
Skip the device validation prompt. For scripted use only: the prompt
exists because the commands below write to raw block devices by path,
and a wrong path destroys whatever is on it.
.TP
.B \-\-scope=quick|standard|full
How much forensic capture
.B analyse
collects.
.TP
.B \-\-injector \fIname\fR
Which fault injector to drive.
.TP
.B \-\-flip\-width \fIn\fR
Bits flipped per event, for multi\-bit upsets.
.TP
.B \-\-script \fIname\fR
Which script
.B trace
attaches, with or without its
.I .bt
suffix. Default
.IR lostptr .
.TP
.B \-\-target \fIname\fR
Where it attaches:
.IR host ,
or a cluster node by hostname or address. Default
.IR compute01 .
.TP
.B \-\-seconds \fIn\fR
How long the probe stays attached. It is stopped with INT rather than
KILL, so bpftrace prints its maps on the way out.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the scripts found, with the first line of each one's header, and
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-bitbake
Use the image already built rather than rebuilding. Only when nothing in
the sources has changed: the lockstep check will catch a tree that has,
but not a stale image.
.TP
.B \-\-accept\-regression
Let the pipeline continue past a result worse than the stored one.
Records the acceptance in the manifest.
.TP
.B \-\-linux \fIdir\fR
For
.BR upstream ,
the kernel repository holding the series branch. Default
BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_REPO, or ~/git/linux.
.TP
.B \-\-series \fIbranch\fR
The branch carrying the series. Default
.IR beamfs\-rfc\-v1 .
.TP
.B \-\-base \fIrev\fR
The commit the series is based on. Required.
.TP
.B \-\-measured \fIrev\fR
The beamfs commit whose sources the series carries. Default HEAD.
.TP
.B \-\-same\-text\-as \fIrev\fR
The beamfs commit whose compiled code the series must reproduce: for a
release that changes comments and layout only, the measured release
before it.
.TP
.B \-\-newer \fIrevs\fR
Comma separated revisions the series must merge into and build on.
Default origin/master,linux-next/master; their remotes are fetched first
unless
.BR \-\-no\-fetch .
.TP
.B \-\-subject\-prefix \fIprefix\fR
Default
.IR "RFC PATCH" .
.TP
.B \-\-assisted\-by \fIvalue\fR
The Assisted\-by line every patch carries. Default
.IR LLM ;
empty for none.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-blocking
The \-\-strict CHECKs of checkpatch fail the check as well.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cross
Leave the arm64 build to the bitbake chain.
.TP
.B \-\-stack\-limit \fIbytes\fR
The largest stack frame checkstack accepts. Default 512, the figure of
the submit checklist.
.TP
.B \-\-out \fIdir\fR
Where the run writes, an absolute path. Default
~/.local/share/beamfs\-bench/upstream/<series>\-<stamp>.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_REPO
The kernel repository: where
.B upstream
finds the series, and where the code analysis checks out
BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_BASE for its checkpatch and its kernel\-doc. Default
~/git/linux.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_BENCH_LINUX_BASE
The revision of that repository whose checkpatch and kernel\-doc the
code analysis runs. Default origin/master.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_BENCH_BPF_SCRIPTS
Where
.B trace
looks for scripts first. Without it the search runs through this
repository, the beamfs\-xfstests checkout under the home directory, and
the installed copies of both. A script edited for one run is the normal
case, which is why the environment comes first and nothing needs
reinstalling.
.TP
.B BEAMFS_NO_BELL
Stay quiet for a whole shell. See
.B THE BELL
below.
.SH THE LAB
Four VMs on the hpcnet network: one master at 192.168.56.10 and three
compute nodes at .11 through .13. The master carries a rootfs and the
cluster /data and nothing else; each compute node carries those two plus
five USB sticks and a scratch image.
.PP
That split is the isolation contract, and the pipeline refuses to start
if it does not hold. A USB attached to the master would put a
filesystem under test on the node that coordinates the test.
.PP
The sticks vary by a factor of nineteen in write throughput \(em 2.7 to
51.7 MB/s measured on 2026-09-11 \(em which matters for any comparison
spread across them.
.SH THE BELL
A full pipeline runs for the better part of an hour and ends in a
terminal nobody is watching. Every subcommand that does real work rings
when it is done, every four seconds, until Return is pressed.
.PP
The sound is compiled into the binary. What is needed is a player, and
one of these has to be on PATH:
.PP
.RS
.nf
paplay      pulseaudio-utils, and what pipewire-pulse answers
pw-play     pipewire
aplay       alsa-utils
play        sox
ffplay      ffmpeg
.fi
.RE
.PP
With none of them installed the run is silent and nothing else changes.
It also stays quiet when standard input is not a terminal -- under a
scheduler, or through a pipe -- because there would be nobody to press
Return and the loop would not end. Pass
.B \-\-no\-bell
for a single run, accepted on any subcommand, or set
.B BEAMFS_NO_BELL
for a whole shell, which is what to use when one run is chained into
another or when the sound would come out of the wrong machine.
.PP
When this is packaged, one of those players belongs in the dependencies
as a suggests rather than a requires: the tool works without one.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I Documentation/runs/manifest-*.json
What ran, on what, with what. GPG\-signed, one per run.
.TP
.I /tmp/beamfs-bench-current-run/
The run in progress.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/beamfs\-bench/upstream/
One directory per run of
.BR upstream :
the patches as they would be mailed, the logs, the summary and the
report.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything ran and nothing regressed.
.TP
.B 3
A gate refused: the lab, the code analysis, a regression against the
stored result, or one of the checks of
.BR upstream .
The pipeline stops rather than measuring on a foundation it cannot
vouch for.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR beamfs-xfstests (1),
.BR fsck.beamfs (8)
